Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that utilizes advanced features such as Promises, async/await, generators, and object destructuring:

```javascript
// Function that returns a Promise simulating an API call delay
const fakeApiCall = (data, delay) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(data), delay));

// Async function with a generator to manage multiple API calls
async function* apiDataGenerator() {
    const userData = await fakeApiCall({ name: 'Alice', age: 25 }, 1000);
    yield { userData };

    const postsData = await fakeApiCall([{ id: 1, title: 'Post 1' }, { id: 2, title: 'Post 2' }], 1000);
    yield { postsData };

    const commentsData = await fakeApiCall([{ postId: 1, content: 'Nice!' }, { postId: 2, content: 'Great!' }], 1000);
    yield { commentsData };
}

 
async function processApiData() {
    const dataGenerator = apiDataGenerator();
    
    for await (const { userData, postsData, commentsData } of dataGenerator) {
        if (userData) {
            console.log(`User: ${userData.name}, Age: ${userData.age}`);
        }
        if (postsData) {
            postsData.forEach(post => console.log(`Post: ${post.title}`));
        }
        if (commentsData) {
            commentsData.forEach(comment => console.log(`Comment on Post ${comment.postId}: ${comment.content}`));
        }
    }
}

processApiData().catch(console.error);
```

This program demonstrates a complex use of JavaScript features by simulating sequential API calls using `Promises`, consuming the results with `async/await`, and managing the asynchronous flow using a `generator`. It further utilizes object destructuring to handle multiple data sources.